    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p64-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $P(x)=x^3-8x^2+9x+18$ is divisible by both $(x-3)$ and $(x+1)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(3)=0$ and $P(-1)=0$, so both are factors.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}P(3)&=3^3-8(3)^2+9(3)+18\\\\P(3)&=27-72+27+18=0\\\\P(-1)&=(-1)^3-8(-1)^2+9(-1)+18\\\\P(-1)&=-1-8-9+18=0\\end{aligned}$$\nBy the factor theorem, $(x-3)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $P(x)$, so $P(x)$ is divisible by both.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p64-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "By considering the leading term and constant term, express $P(x)$ as a product of three linear factors and hence solve $P(x)=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(x)=(x-3)(x+1)(x-6)$; $x=3,-1,6$.",
          "worked": "The leading coefficient is $1$, so write $P(x)=(x-3)(x+1)(x+c)$. Comparing constant terms:\n$$\\begin{aligned}(-3)(1)c&=18\\\\c&=-6\\\\P(x)&=(x-3)(x+1)(x-6)\\end{aligned}$$\nBy the null factor law, $P(x)=0$ gives $x=3,-1,6$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
